  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/ci/image33-0f190c28-f4e4-42f5-b051-f0a89d3bb367.jpg\" style=\"height: 118px; width: 200px;\" /><br /><br />Let us assume that the charge flowing from earth to the sphere is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. Since the sphere is grounded, its potential will be zero. Hence,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn mathvariant=\"italic\">0</mn></msub><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>q</mi></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn mathvariant=\"italic\">0</mn></msub><mi>d</mi></mstyle></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mi>d</mi></mstyle></mfrac></math></p>",
